It looks like you're asking about the difference between Singleton, Scoped, and Transient lifetimes in ASP.NET Core Dependency Injection.
Dependency Injection Lifetimes
| Lifetime      | Created                         | Shared                    | Use Case                        |
| ------------- | ------------------------------- | ------------------------- | ------------------------------- |
| **Transient** | Every time it's requested       | ❌ No                      | Lightweight, stateless services |
| **Scoped**    | Once per HTTP request           | ✅ Within the same request | Database access, Unit of Work   |
| **Singleton** | Once for the entire application | ✅ Across all requests     | Configuration, caching, logging |

1. Transient
A new instance is created every time the service is requested.

builder.Services.AddTransient<IEmailService, EmailService>();
Example

public class EmailService
{
    public Guid Id { get; } = Guid.NewGuid();
}

If injected twice:

var s1 = emailService1.Id;
var s2 = emailService2.Id;

Output:

s1 = A123
s2 = B456

Different objects every time.

Use when
Email service
PDF generator
Validation service
Helper classes


2. Scoped
One instance is created per HTTP request.

builder.Services.AddScoped<IProductRepository, ProductRepository>();

During one request:

Controller
    ↓
Repository
    ↓
Service

All receive the same object.

Request 1

Repository = 123
Service = 123

Request 2

Repository = 789
Service = 789
Use when
DbContext
Repository Pattern
Unit of Work
Business services

Entity Framework DbContext should always be Scoped.

3. Singleton

Created only once when the application starts.

builder.Services.AddSingleton<ICacheService, CacheService>();

Every request uses the same object.

Request 1

CacheService = 999

Request 2

CacheService = 999

Request 100

CacheService = 999

Always the same instance.

Use when
Memory cache
Configuration
Logging
Application settings
